    front_text: 'What is electrical stimulation for pelvic floor dysfunction?',
    back_text: 'PELVIC FLOOR ELECTRICAL STIMULATION:\n\nTYPES:\n\n1. INTRAVAGINAL/INTRARECTAL:\n- Direct stimulation of PFM\n- Used for strengthening\n- Also inhibits detrusor\n\n2. SURFACE ELECTRODES:\n- Perineal/sacral placement\n- Less invasive option\n\n3. PERCUTANEOUS TIBIAL NERVE STIMULATION (PTNS):\n- Needle near tibial nerve\n- Affects sacral plexus\n- Used for overactive bladder\n\n4. SACRAL NEUROMODULATION:\n- Implanted device\n- For refractory cases\n\nPARAMETERS:\n- Stress incontinence: 35-50 Hz\n- Urge incontinence: 5-20 Hz\n- Pain: 2-10 Hz\n\nINDICATIONS:\n- Weak pelvic floor muscles\n- Poor muscle awareness\n- Overactive bladder\n- Fecal incontinence\n- Chronic pelvic pain\n\nCONTRAINDICATIONS:\n- Pregnancy\n- Pacemaker (relative)\n- Active infection',
    source_pdf_id: null,
    source_page_number: null,
    tags: ['electrical stimulation', 'pelvic floor'],
  },
  {
    id: 'fc-gigu-17',
    subcategory_id: 'gigu-incontinence',
    front_text: 'What is fecal incontinence and its management?',
    back_text: 'FECAL INCONTINENCE:\nInvoluntary loss of solid/liquid stool or gas\n\nCAUSES:\n- Anal sphincter damage (obstetric injury)\n- Neurological (SCI, stroke, MS, diabetes)\n- Rectal prolapse\n- IBD/diarrhea\n- Cognitive impairment\n- Fecal impaction with overflow\n\nTYPES:\n- Passive: No awareness of leakage\n- Urge: Cannot delay defecation\n- Seepage: Staining after normal BM\n\nASSESSMENT:\n- Bowel diary\n- Digital rectal exam\n- Anorectal manometry\n\nTREATMENT:\n- Dietary modification (fiber, avoid triggers)\n- Bowel schedule/regulation\n- Pelvic floor exercises\n- Biofeedback (coordination training)\n- Electrical stimulation\n- Medications (antidiarrheals)\n- Sacral neuromodulation\n- Surgical options',
    source_pdf_id: null,
    source_page_number: null,
    tags: ['fecal incontinence', 'bowel'],
  },
  {
    id: 'fc-gigu-18',
    subcategory_id: 'gigu-bladder-bowel',
    front_text: 'What is intermittent catheterization and its guidelines?',
    back_text: 'CLEAN INTERMITTENT CATHETERIZATION (CIC):\n\nINDICATIONS:\n- Neurogenic bladder\n- Urinary retention\n- Post-void residual >100-150 mL\n- Post-surgical\n\nFREQUENCY:\n- Every 4-6 hours (4-6 times/day)\n- Keep bladder volume <400-500 mL\n- Adjust based on output\n\nTECHNIQUE:\n- Clean hands (not sterile at home)\n- Lubricate catheter\n- Insert until urine flows\n- Drain completely\n- Remove slowly\n\nCATHETER TYPES:\n- Straight catheters\n- Hydrophilic (pre-lubricated)\n- Closed system\n\nCOMPLICATIONS:\n- UTI (most common)\n- Urethral trauma\n- False passage\n- Bladder stones\n\nPREVENTION:\n- Adequate fluid intake\n- Regular schedule\n- Proper technique',
    source_pdf_id: null,
    source_page_number: null,
    tags: ['catheterization', 'bladder management'],
  },
  {
    id: 'fc-gigu-19',
    subcategory_id: 'gigu-pelvic-floor',
    front_text: 'What are pelvic floor considerations during pregnancy and postpartum?',
    back_text: 'PREGNANCY PELVIC FLOOR CHANGES:\n\n- Increased weight/pressure on PFM\n- Hormonal changes (relaxin)\n- Ligament laxity\n- Postural changes\n- Risk of stress incontinence (30-50%)\n\nVAGINAL DELIVERY EFFECTS:\n- Pudendal nerve stretch\n- Muscle/connective tissue damage\n- Anal sphincter injury (3rd/4th degree tear)\n- Episiotomy\n\nPOSTPARTUM:\n- Begin Kegels early (if no pain)\n- Wait 6 weeks for internal exam\n- Progress gradually\n- Address diastasis recti first if present\n\nRISK FACTORS FOR PFD:\n- Prolonged pushing\n- Forceps/vacuum delivery\n- Large baby (>4 kg)\n- Episiotomy\n- Previous PFD\n- Multiparity\n\nPREVENTION:\n- Prenatal pelvic floor exercises\n- Perineal massage (36+ weeks)\n- Proper pushing technique\n- Postpartum rehab',
    source_pdf_id: null,
    source_page_number: null,
    tags: ['pregnancy', 'postpartum', 'pelvic floor'],
  },
  {
    id: 'fc-gigu-20',
    subcategory_id: 'gigu-incontinence',
    front_text: 'What behavioral strategies are used for incontinence management?',
    back_text: 'BEHAVIORAL STRATEGIES FOR INCONTINENCE:\n\n1. BLADDER DIARY:\n- Track intake, output, leakage\n- 3-7 days\n- Identifies patterns\n\n2. FLUID MANAGEMENT:\n- 6-8 glasses/day (not less)\n- Avoid bladder irritants\n- Limit before bed\n\n3. TIMED VOIDING:\n- Scheduled bathroom trips\n- Every 2-4 hours\n- Prevents overdistension\n\n4. PROMPTED VOIDING (for cognitively impaired):\n- Caregiver prompts to toilet\n- Regular schedule\n- Positive reinforcement\n\n5. URGE SUPPRESSION:\n- Quick PFM contractions\n- Stay still\n- Distraction\n- Deep breathing\n\n6. LIFESTYLE MODIFICATIONS:\n- Weight loss\n- Smoking cessation\n- Treat constipation\n- Avoid heavy lifting\n\n7. ENVIRONMENTAL MODIFICATIONS:\n- Clear path to bathroom\n- Adequate lighting\n- Bedside commode\n- Raised toilet seat',
    source_pdf_id: null,
    source_page_number: null,
    tags: ['incontinence', 'behavioral', 'management'],
