    front_text: 'What are the common types of AFOs (Ankle-Foot Orthoses)?',
    back_text: 'AFO TYPES:\n\n1. SOLID AFO:\n- Fixed ankle (usually slight DF)\n- Maximum stability\n- Blocks PF and DF\n- For: Severe weakness, spasticity\n\n2. POSTERIOR LEAF SPRING (PLS) AFO:\n- Thin posterior strut\n- Allows DF, blocks PF\n- Provides toe clearance during swing\n- For: Foot drop with mild spasticity\n\n3. HINGED AFO:\n- Ankle joint allows DF\n- Blocks PF during swing\n- Allows ankle motion during stance\n- For: Moderate weakness, need for DF\n\n4. FLOOR REACTION AFO:\n- Anterior shell design\n- Creates knee extension moment\n- For: Crouch gait, quad weakness\n\n5. ARTICULATING AFO:\n- Adjustable joint\n- Can set PF/DF stops\n- Customizable motion\n\n6. SUPRAMALLEOLAR ORTHOSIS (SMO):\n- Below ankle\n- Controls hindfoot, not ankle\n- For: Mild instability, pronation',
