    front_text: 'What are common transtibial prosthetic gait deviations?',
    back_text: 'TRANSTIBIAL PROSTHETIC GAIT DEVIATIONS:\n\nEXCESSIVE KNEE FLEXION (Stance):\n- Causes: Socket too far anterior, foot too dorsiflexed\n- Heel too soft, weak quads\n\nGENU RECURVATUM:\n- Causes: Socket too posterior, foot too plantarflexed\n- Heel too stiff, weak quads\n\nLATERAL TRUNK LEAN (to prosthetic side):\n- Causes: Prosthesis too short, socket too small\n- Weak hip abductors, pain in residual limb\n\nCIRCUMDUCTION:\n- Causes: Prosthesis too long, locked knee\n- Inadequate suspension, weak hip flexors\n\nVAULTING:\n- Causes: Prosthesis too long, inadequate suspension\n- Excessive PF in foot\n\nWIDE BOS:\n- Causes: Prosthesis too long, pelvic instability\n- Socket discomfort\n\nUNEQUAL STEP LENGTH:\n- Causes: Socket discomfort, fear\n- Poor proprioception, alignment issues',
